course: dictionary
lesson: 3765
title: Fog: a word with two faces
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fog is a thick cloud of tiny water drops near the ground that makes it hard to see. «Fog and dark can make even a short walk confusing, so hikers stay together and tell someone their route.» The clues are confusing, stay together and tell someone their route. Those all tell you that fog makes it hard to see where you are going.
explain: Fog can also mean a state of confusion. If someone says you are «in a fog», they mean your thoughts are muddled and you cannot think clearly. The word is a noun here, a thing you can be in, just as you can be in a cloud.
explain: Fog is also a verb, an action word. «My glasses fogged up when I came in from the cold.» Try the test: swap in «My glasses were covered with tiny drops and I could not see through them» and the sentence still makes sense, so fogged up means that.
fact: Fog is really a cloud at ground level. It forms when moist air cools near the ground.
fact: In thick fog, drivers slow down and use fog lights, and walkers wear bright clothes so they can be seen.
fact: «The fog-basking beetle of the Namib Desert» stands head-down on a sand dune at dawn. Fog forms droplets on its bumpy back, and they roll down to its mouth.
fact: Engineers have copied the beetle's back to collect water from fog.
fact: Phrases to know: thick fog, fog lights, fog horn, fog up, in a fog.
exercise: «Fog and dark can make even a short walk confusing.» What does fog mean in this sentence? | A bright light that helps cars see | A loud sound from a ship | A hot, dry wind from the desert | *A thick cloud of tiny water drops near the ground
exercise: Dad says, «I am still in a fog after that late night.» What does in a fog mean? | *Confused and unable to think clearly | Very happy and full of energy | Lost on a hill | Covered in tiny water drops
exercise: «My glasses fogged up when I came in from the cold.» Find and copy the word that tells you the glasses became hard to see through. | *fogged | glasses | came | cold
test: In thick fog, drivers slow down and use fog lights. Why do drivers use fog lights? | To wake up the driver | *So they can be seen and see the road better | To make the car go faster | To keep the car warm
test: «The fog-basking beetle stands on a dune at dawn, and fog forms droplets on its bumpy back.» Where do the droplets roll to? | Into the sand | *Down to its mouth | Into its eyes | Up into the sky
exam: «The ship's horn sounded through the fog all morning.» What does fog mean in this sentence? | Heavy rain falling on the sea | *A thick cloud of tiny water drops near the ground | A state of confusion | A bright light on the ship
